Below is a step‑by‑step guide you can follow (or give to a developer) to add Stripe payments to your **Claude Code** + **Next.js** project using the `react-easy-stripe-checkout-pro` component.

---

## 1.  Install the package

```bash
# From the root of your Next.js project
yarn add react-easy-stripe-checkout-pro   # or
npm i react-easy-stripe-checkout-pro
```

---

## 2.  Create a helper file for Stripe

Create a file so you don’t need to import `react-easy-stripe-checkout-pro` in every component.

```tsx
// lib/Stripe.tsx
import { useEffect, useState } from "react";
import StripeCheckout from "react-easy-stripe-checkout-pro";

interface Props {
  amount: number;              // in *cents* (e.g. $10.00 => 1000)
  currency?: string;
  name?: string;
  description?: string;
  stripeKey?: string;          // publishable key
  onToken: (token: any) => void;  // handler called after successful tokenization
  children?: React.ReactNode; // override button if you want
}

export default function StripeButton({
  amount,
  currency = "USD",
  name = "Product",
  description = "Purchase",
  stripeKey = process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY!,
  onToken,
  children,
}: Props) {
  const [show, setShow] = useState(false);

  return (
    <>
      {/* Render whatever triggers the modal (defaults to a button) */}
      {children ?? (
        <button className="btn" onClick={() => setShow(true)}>
          Pay
        </button>
      )}

      {/* Modal */}
      {show && (
        <StripeCheckout
          name={name}
          description={description}
          amount={amount}
          stripeKey={stripeKey}
          token={onToken}
          currency={currency}
          open={show}
          close={() => setShow(false)}
        />
      )}
    </>
  );
}
```

> **Why this helper?**  
> 1. Keeps your component tree clean.  
> 2. Lets you change styling or add more props later without touching every button.  
> 3. Makes it easy to replace the Stripe library in the future.

---

## 3.  Create a checkout page or component

Let’s build a simple “Buy now” page that uses the helper.

```tsx
// pages/checkout.tsx
import { useRouter } from "next/router";
import StripeButton from "../lib/Stripe";

const CheckoutPage = () => {
  const router = useRouter();

  // 1. when your customer clicks “Pay”, Stripe Checkout shows up.
  // 2. after they finish, Stripe returns a Token, we send that to our API.
  const handleToken = async (token: any) => {
    try {
      const res = await fetch("/api/stripe/charge", {
        method: "POST",
        headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
        body: JSON.stringify({
          token: token.id,
          amount: 2000, // $20.00  (in cents)
        }),
      });

      if (res.ok) {
        router.push("/success");
      } else {
        const err = await res.json();
        alert(err.message || "Payment failed");
      }
    } catch (err) {
      console.error(err);
      alert("An unexpected error occurred");
    }
  };

  return (
    <div className="max-w-3xl mx-auto p-8">
      <h1 className="text-3xl mb-4">Purchase Our Premium Plan</h1>
      <p className="mb-6">Secure payment with Stripe.</p>

      <StripeButton
        amount={2000}          // 2000 cents = $20.00
        name="Premium Plan"
        description="Pay $20.00 upfront"
        onToken={handleToken}
        stripeKey={process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY!}
      />
    </div>
  );
};

export default CheckoutPage;
```

---

## 4.  Add a secure server‑side route to create a charge

Create a new API route inside `pages/api/stripe/charge.ts`.  
(You’ll need a Stripe secret key in `/.env.local`.)

```ts
// pages/api/stripe/charge.ts
import { NextApiRequest, NextApiResponse } from "next";
import Stripe from "stripe";

const stripe = new Stripe(process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY!, {
  apiVersion: "2024-04-10", // use the latest stable version
});

export default async function handler(
  req: NextApiRequest,
  res: NextApiResponse
) {
  if (req.method !== "POST") {
    res.setHeader("Allow", "POST");
    return res.status(405).end("Method Not Allowed");
  }

  const { token, amount } = req.body;

  try {
    const customer = await stripe.customers.create({
      email: token.email,
      source: token.id,
    });

    const charge = await stripe.charges.create({
      amount,            // amount in cents
      currency: "usd",
      customer: customer.id,
      description: "Purchase from Claude Code",
    });

    res.status(200).json({ id: charge.id, status: charge.status });
  } catch (error: any) {
    console.error("Stripe error:", error);
    res.status(500).json({ message: error.message || "Server error" });
  }
}
```

**Environment variables (add to `.env.local`):**

```dotenv
# publishable key – can be accessed by the client
NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=pk_test_XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX

# secret key – used only on the server
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_REDACTED
```

> **Tip:** Never put the secret key in client‑side code; always keep it server‑only!

